title: v0.1a: the first release, packs as plain folders
---
date: 2026-04-06
---
author: Labyricorn
---
summary: The foundation: file-system packs, bindings, CSS-variable controls, fire events, and three local servers.
---
tags: architecture, local-first, html
---
source_commit: c78872fc6067a5ecdb5b08d574037b535325259e
---
body:

TitleFlow's first release set the shape the project still has. A pack is an
immediate subfolder of `packs/`, and each element inside it is a folder holding
`element.html`, `element.css`, and an optional `element.js`. No configuration
file is required, because the folder structure describes itself.

Editable behavior is declared in the markup and styles rather than in a separate
schema:

- Text fields come from `data-tf-bind` and `data-tf-label` attributes.
- Dock controls come from `--tf-ui-*` CSS variables, with a color, duration,
  length, or text control inferred from the value.
- `show` and `hide` fire events drive entrances and exits, and each dispatches a
  `tf:fire` event that an element's script can handle.

Three local servers split the work. The runtime serves the composed overlay to an
OBS browser source and streams changes over server-sent events. The editor offers
code panels and a preview that uses the same composition logic. The dock is the
operator's control surface. A validator checks slugs, required files, binding
pairs, variable names, and broad selectors, and a starter lower-third pack
shipped as a working example.

The release also listed its limits plainly: dock edits did not yet persist,
OBS needed a manual refresh after a pack switch, and CSS validation was textual.

This entry was written from the project's changelog. The repository's history
begins with a later snapshot commit, which it references.

[Commit c78872f](https://github.com/Labyricorn/TitleFlow/commit/c78872fc6067a5ecdb5b08d574037b535325259e)
